# 2026-01-27 – Project 08 – Nginx HTTPS (Self-Signed)

## Summary

Today I configured HTTPS on Nginx using a self-signed SSL certificate on my Ubuntu MacBook.

The objective was to understand how TLS works at the web server level, including certificate generation, secure listeners on port 443, and validating HTTPS using both command-line tools and a web browser.

This project builds directly on my previous Nginx installation and reverse proxy work.

---

## What I Implemented

- Generated a self-signed SSL certificate
- Configured Nginx to listen on port 443 (HTTPS)
- Served a static site securely from `/var/www/project08`
- Enabled and validated HTTPS using `curl` and a web browser
- Verified active listeners and enabled sites
- Debugged initial port 443 connection issues

---

## What I Validated

- Nginx configuration syntax using `nginx -t`
- Active listeners using `ss -tulpn`
- HTTPS connectivity using `curl -k https://localhost`
- Browser access with expected self-signed certificate warning

---

## Issues & Fixes

Initially, HTTPS failed because Nginx was not listening on port 443.

Instead of assuming a firewall issue, I verified:
- Enabled Nginx sites
- Active listeners
- Configuration syntax

Once the HTTPS site was enabled and Nginx reloaded, the service worked as expected.

---

## Key Takeaways

- HTTPS requires an explicit listener on port 443
- Certificates must be correctly referenced in Nginx configs
- Most HTTPS issues are configuration-related, not firewall-related
- Self-signed certificates are ideal for lab environments

